The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```java
private static final Map<ClassLoader, Map<String, Class<?>>> CACHE_CLASSES = new WeakHashMap<ClassLoader, Map<String, Class<?>>>();
Class<?> clazz = map.get(name);
```
Reason:
The original implementation stored strong references to class objects in the `CACHE_CLASSES` map, which prevented the garbage collector from reclaiming memory for classes that were no longer needed. This led to memory leaks as the cached class references remained in memory indefinitely.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
private static final Map<ClassLoader, Map<String, WeakReference<Class<?>>> CACHE_CLASSES = new WeakHashMap<ClassLoader, Map<String, WeakReference<Class<?>>>>();
WeakReference<Class<?>> ref = map.get(name); 
clazz = ref.get();
```
Reason:
The patch changes the `CACHE_CLASSES` map to store `WeakReference<Class<?>>` instead of strong references. This allows the garbage collector to reclaim memory for class objects when they are no longer in use, thus preventing memory leaks. The retrieval of the class now checks the weak reference, allowing for proper garbage collection.

